  "articleBody": "Wondering if your website is truly equipped for the age of AI search and voice assistants? It's a common question, and getting an honest answer is crucial. Many businesses find their sites, while great for human visitors, fall short when it comes to AI's specific needs. AI systems look for structured data, clean HTML, and content that directly answers questions. They often get tripped up by heavy JavaScript, outdated FAQs, or vital information buried in images.\n\n- [ ] Check robots.txt and noindex directives — why it matters: These files tell AI crawlers what to access and what to ignore. Blocking essential content means AI can't read it, plain and simple.\n- [ ] Assess visible text availability — why it matters: AI primarily understands text. If your key information is only in images or dynamic elements, AI can't process it to give answers.\n- [ ] Validate structured data (JSON-LD) — why it matters: While not strictly required for AI, accurate schema helps AI understand your content's context and meaning, making it easier for them to cite you.\n- [ ] Review page titles, descriptions, and headings — why it matters: These elements signal to AI what your page is about and often provide concise answers for snippets.\n- [ ] Monitor index coverage in webmaster tools — why it matters: This shows you what pages Google and other search engines are actually finding and adding to their index, giving you a baseline for AI visibility.\n- [ ] Confirm mobile usability — why it matters: A mobile-friendly site is a crawl-friendly site. AI crawlers expect a good user experience, and poor mobile performance can signal content quality issues.\n\nHow to use this checklist: Go through each item for your most important service pages. If you find multiple 'no's or don't understand an item, it might be time to get some expert eyes on your site. This isn't just about search rankings anymore; it's about being the answer.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-23"
}